  "argument": "That situation is completely unacceptable and not how the academic recommendation system should function. When professors cannot write a strong letter for a student, the proper approach is to decline or write only factual, neutral statements—such as listing courses taken or projects completed—which signals to committees that there isn't enthusiastic support without being explicitly negative.

You have two options: either stop using this professor as a reference going forward, or address the issue directly with her. If you choose to confront it, consider sending a professionally worded email expressing your confusion. You might write something like: \"Dear Dr. [Name], I am currently applying to graduate programs and asked you to serve as a reference based on what I believed was a positive working relationship. However, during a recent interview, an admissions committee member informed me that your letter did not recommend me for the program, citing [specific reasons mentioned]. I'm surprised and concerned by this, as these issues were never brought to my attention during our work together or when I requested your recommendation. I would appreciate understanding what occurred, as this has significantly affected my graduate school prospects.\""
